module qualified_arm.pos_1

// A QUALIFIED VARIANT AS A MATCH-ARM PATTERN: `Kind.A => ...`, the sum named
// before the variant. Both compilers must accept it and print the same thing.
//
// WHY THIS FORM AND NOT ANOTHER, measured 2026-09-20. After the peers-as-a-unit
// wave, 332 diagnostics of self-hosting remained, and 143 of them were the
// parser's fallback -- a BUCKET rather than a form. Opened the way plan
// 274:3056 prescribes, by the FIRST refusal of each file (later spans are
// recovery debris), it came to nineteen places, and they were one family: the
// patterns of match arms. A matrix of one shape per file named the four the
// parser stopped at, and this is the first of them, worth 35 diagnostics of the
// remainder on its own.
//
// THE OTHER THREE ARE NAMED HERE so the next reader does not take this file for
// the whole of the work: a bare `return` as an arm body, a string literal arm,
// and a char literal arm. They are separate fixtures when they land.
//
// BOTH SPELLINGS IN ONE PROGRAM on purpose. `Kind.A` and a bare `A` mean the same
// variant, and a fixture carrying only the qualified one would pass just as
// well if the parser had quietly started reading a qualified name as something
// else entirely -- a field access, say. Printing both and getting the same
// answer is what makes the form, and not merely its acceptance, the subject.

type Kind enum A | B

fn name_of(k Kind) -> str => match k {
    Kind.A => "A"
    Kind.B => "B"
}

fn bare_of(k Kind) -> str => match k {
    A => "A"
    B => "B"
}

fn main() {
    println(name_of(Kind.A))
    println(bare_of(Kind.B))
    println("${name_of(Kind.B) == bare_of(Kind.B)}")
}
